-# Frontier
+# Frontier — planetary cloud transport
+
+This checkout (`streamlinkinbox/Frontier`) contained only a README. The native engine lives in [SultanAladin/Frontier-1](https://github.com/SultanAladin/Frontier-1). This branch carries the new cloud modules, a patch for the native engine, and reproducible offscreen renders. It does **not** contain a second copy of that engine or modify its remote branch. The integration target is `arena/39c6910b-frontier-1` at `8d75f4af29ffa800ea39ad58c9128673aa000ea1`.
+
+## Apply to the native engine
+
+From this checkout, with a separate Frontier-1 checkout at the reference revision:
+
+```sh
+python3 Automation/ApplyFrontierCloudPatch.py /path/to/Frontier-1 --check
+python3 Automation/ApplyFrontierCloudPatch.py /path/to/Frontier-1
+```
+
+The installer supplies the same four modules used by the proof renderer, along with the edits in `patches/Frontier1CloudIntegration.patch`. It checks the complete patch before writing. Use the target's pinned dependency setup and CMake build after applying. Set `Atmosphere.Medium.AirlessBody = true` for a lunar scene; this overrides authored cloud and atmospheric fog settings. This is a scene configuration flag, not a new editor control.
+
+### What changed
+
+- **Shape:** a nonperiodic, domain-warped planetary moisture, type, uplift and precipitation field. Radius-preserving vortex turns avoid folding storm fronts into repeated rings. Convective cells collect in variable banks with two-lobe rounded profiles. Broken mid-level sheets and sheared, anisotropic high ice use independent profiles. A deterministic 128³ Perlin–Worley base, 64³ Worley erosion volume and boundary curl supply local detail. Only the local noise textures wrap; the weather field does not.
+- **Transport:** cloud shells intersect the actual planet-relative camera ray. Downward and interior rays work, foreground surfaces clip the integration, and a continuous footprint blend replaces the old separate orbital cloud mask. The march uses a void-and-cluster blue-noise tile, short steps near the camera, refinement on re-entry into condensate, dry-interval skipping, and no accumulated cloud frames.
+- **Light:** dual-lobe HG, normalized scattering-order weights with a powder-dependent redistribution, sky and horizon bounce, and directional Beer attenuation across the three decks. The same attenuation query serves orbital ground shading, world surfaces, and the existing god-ray pass. Planetary occlusion is evaluated at the sample, so the upper deck can retain direct light at civil twilight. The aerial composite restores the in-scattered light in front of the cloud instead of dimming all the horizon haze by cloud opacity.
+- **Integration:** full-resolution clouds by default; no extra five-tap cloud-buffer blur. World terrain and authored local volumes keep their original renderer paths, with the new global volume integrated at their boundaries. The Vulkan integration retains the spatial wind probes and sends moving primary-sky clouds to the current-frame composite rather than path-trace history.
+
+### Limits
+
+This is a **renderer candidate, not a photorealism sign-off**. The global change targets the native Vulkan renderer. It leaves Frontier-1's legacy CPU fallback density and the dedicated local-volume density model in place. The specialized global formation presets still control deck geometry, but their old bespoke masks are not reproduced one-for-one. Independent legacy shadow-shape knobs no longer deform the global shadow field separately from visible density. Directional quadrature and the optical-centroid aerial composite are approximations; there is no baked deep shadow map, real weather assimilation, or claimed frame-rate target. There was no physical GPU in the verification environment.
+
+## Reproduce the captures
+
+The exhibit runs the **same Slang cloud functions** as the integration patch. It uses a small procedural ocean and an Earth surface-only texture for framing. Every cloud pixel is rendered by the shader. The checked-in images used Slang 2026.14.1's native C++ compute target, dispatched offscreen on the CPU, with no upscaler or generative images. They are not screenshots of the full Frontier editor.
+
+Requirements: Linux, Python 3.10+, Pillow, a C++20 compiler, and `slangc` 2026.14.1 (the version bundled by Frontier-1's `Automation/PrepareShaderToolchain.py`). The optional `--backend vulkan` and `TestCloudProof.py --vulkan-diagnostics` additionally need the Python `vulkan` package, a Vulkan loader, and a suitable compute device. The diagnostics dispatch the math and the packed volumes without the full beauty-ray pipeline. Add `--frontier-checkout /path/to/patched/Frontier-1` to the test command for a separate host smoke test of the actual viewport cloud entry point and its uniform layout.
+
+```sh
+python3 -m pip install Pillow
+SLANGC=/path/to/slangc
+# Used only as the cloud-free *surface* in the orbit scene.
+gh api repos/mrdoob/three.js/git/blobs/b9410c7bea22e43c368b0757582160d0cf4cff03 \
+  --jq .content | base64 --decode > /tmp/earth-day.jpg
+python3 Automation/TestCloudProof.py --slangc "$SLANGC"
+python3 Automation/RenderCloudProof.py ground sunset dawn orbit --slangc "$SLANGC" \
+  --earth /tmp/earth-day.jpg --width 1600 --height 900 --steps 128 --light-steps 4
+python3 Automation/RenderCloudProof.py gap --slangc "$SLANGC" \
+  --width 1600 --height 900 --steps 128 --light-steps 4 --samples 2
+```
+
+Supply scene names (`ground`, `gap`, `sunset`, `dawn`, `orbit`) to render just those frames. `--transmittance` emits the raw cloud-only transmittance for diagnostics; `moon` with high authored coverage must remain white in that mode. The proof script checks the surface texture against its pinned SHA-256. The Earth day map is by [Solar System Scope](https://www.solarsystemscope.com/textures/) ([CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/)), redistributed by three.js in `examples/textures/planets/earth_day_4096.jpg`. The asset is not stored in this repo.
+
+See [the capture review](output/README.md) for what the images do and do not establish.
