+# Cloud capture review
+
+**Visual result: candidate, not passed.** These captures exercise the new transport, but they do not meet the requested indistinguishable-from-photography bar. They are direct 1600 × 900 offscreen renders from Slang 2026.14.1’s C++ compute target, not editor screenshots, reference photos, or upscaled previews. Each used 128 requested view steps with adaptive allocation and four solar samples per span. The near-sun view uses two subpixel rays per pixel; the other views use one.
+
+| View | Capture | Inspection |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Low ocean angle | [01 — Ocean cumulus](01-ocean-cumulus.png) | The cooler undersides separate from the sunlit edges and the distant clouds fade into the horizon. Several low clouds still have similar silhouettes, and the higher sheets lack fine cirrus detail. |
+| Near-sun gap | [02 — Silver lining](02-silver-lining.png) | Backlit rims separate the nearer cloud bank from the higher deck. The two-sample recapture reduced stipple, but some grain remains at the bottom and the brightest parts lose billow detail. |
+| Golden hour | [03 — Sunset](03-golden-hour.png) | The lower exposure retains more detail around the sun and separates the warm rim from the shaded cloud. The edges remain too soft in places, and the ocean reflection is stylized. |
+| Civil dawn | [04 — Dawn](04-dawn.png) | The pink upper layer stays lit above the cool lower deck. Removing the periodic cirrus lookup got rid of the most conspicuous fine comb streaks, but there is still some soft detail and stipple at the horizon. |
+| Orbit | [05 — Orbital limb](05-orbital-limb.png) | The curved limb, coherent fronts and surface shadows are visible. The revised vortex removed much of the fine striping from the first orbital pass; broad cloud decks still look too flat and uniform. |
+
+## Verification scope
+
+- The installer passed `git apply --check` and applied to an export of Frontier-1 at `8d75f4af29ffa800ea39ad58c9128673aa000ea1`. All 14 applied files matched the validation checkout byte for byte. The three edited C++ translation units and the integration Slang entry points compiled in the Development build.
+- **Slang host execution:** shell intersections (below, inside, tangent, and orbital), non-overlapping decks, reversible weather-front rotation, phase normalization, twilight illumination, zero-coverage bypass, lunar vacuum, and wind displacement with stateless frame replay all passed.
+- **Patched viewport entry on the Slang host:** the 4,144-byte uniform layout, exact airless bypass, and finite cloudy/clear outputs passed through Frontier-1's `CloudComputeEntry`. This does not exercise the Vulkan engine host.
+- **SwiftShader Vulkan diagnostics:** shell intersections, twilight illumination, phase normalization, layer bounds, reversible weather-front rotation, and real reads from the base, erosion and blue-noise volumes all passed. The full proof and native viewport Vulkan pipelines were stopped during software pipeline creation; no GPU beauty dispatch, linked engine run, or frame-rate validation is claimed.
+
+The exhibit uses a procedural ocean and a cloud-free Earth albedo. The shader produces all cloud coverage, opacity and cloud shadows in these frames. See the [reproduction instructions and Earth asset credit](../README.md#reproduce-the-captures).
